ames>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Keeling</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Hennessy</surname><given-names>TP</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A comparison of multimodality therapy and surgery for esophageal adenocarcinoma</article-title><source>N Engl J Med</source><year>1996</year><volume>335</volume><fpage>462</fpage><lpage>467</lpage><!--PubMed citation query: 'N Engl J Med||335|462||bib41|'--><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8672151</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="fig1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Flow diagram illustrating the study conduct and patient outcome according to histopathological response.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="eps" xlink:href="6604659f1"/></fig><fig id="fig2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Survival by histopathological response (<italic>P</italic> (log-rank)=0.21 <italic>vs</italic> non-response). Survival is shown for all patients who underwent surgery (<italic>n</italic>=25). The median OS from date of surgery for histopathological responders was 16 months (95&#x00025; confidence interval (CI): 6.2&#x02013;25.8 months), respectively, compared with 12 months (95&#x00025; CI: 3.2&#x02013;20.8 months) in histopathological non-responders, hazard ratio: 1.78 (95&#x00025; CI: 0.70&#x02013;4.57).</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="eps" xlink:href="6604659f2"/></fig><fig id="fig3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Event-free survival (EFS) by histopathological response (<italic>P</italic> (log-rank)=0.11). EFS is shown for all patients who underwent surgery (<italic>n</italic>=25). The median EFS from the date of surgery was 12 months in histopathological responders (95&#x00025; confidence interval (CI): 6.1&#x02013;17.9 months) <italic>vs</italic> 9 months for non-responders (95&#x00025; CI: 0.3&#x02013;17.8 months).</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.or